DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This  macro can help programmers find bugs in their programs, or handle
       exceptional cases via a crash that will produce limited debugging  out‐
       put.

       If  _e_x_p_r_e_s_s_i_o_n is false (i.e., compares equal to zero), aasssseerrtt() prints
       an error message to standard error and terminates the program by  call‐
       ing  aabboorrtt(3).   The  error  message  includes the name of the file and
       function containing the aasssseerrtt() call, the source code line  number  of
       the call, and the text of the argument; something like:

           prog: some_file.c:16: some_func: Assertion `val == 0' failed.

       If  the  macro  NNDDEEBBUUGG is defined at the moment _<_a_s_s_e_r_t_._h_> was last in‐
       cluded, the macro aasssseerrtt() generates no code, and hence does nothing at
       all.  It is not recommended to define NNDDEEBBUUGG if using aasssseerrtt()  to  de‐
       tect  error  conditions since the software may behave non‐deterministi‐
       cally.

